WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.310
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Daniel Levitin, who's a psychologist,

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:08.420
neuroscientist, author and professor emeritus
at McGill University.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.700
Some of his books include A Field Guide to
Lies, Critical Thinking with Statistics and

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.210
the Scientific Method, and also Weaponised
Lies How to Think Critically in the Post-Truth

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.210
Era.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:20.240
It's really great having you on.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.240
I appreciate it.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.290
Speaker 4: Thank you for having me, David.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:25.460
Speaker 1: So, I mean, where to start?

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.460
Where to start?

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.420
You know, I think maybe one place that would
be interesting would be for context.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:37.649
You know, we've recently been doing a lot
of thinking about how to best engage in conversations

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:43.589
with people who might have different opinions,
but also importantly, who maybe don't have

00:00:43.589 --> 00:00:49.389
a factual foundation or basis on which we
can have those conversations or people who

00:00:49.389 --> 00:00:54.690
can't tell the difference between news reporting
and conspiracy articles that might be shared

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.620
on Facebook as just general examples.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:04.759
One approach is to speak to these folks with
like a Socratic type method of questioning,

00:01:04.759 --> 00:01:08.710
gently probing How did you come to this conclusion?

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.270
How do you determine if a news source is trustworthy?

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.750
It's a it's a very long process, admittedly,
which sometimes is effective at changing minds

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.829
and sometimes is not where maybe we would
start with you.

00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:24.390
As can you talk a little bit about whether
you believe that that's an ideal framework

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.799
for some of these conversations or whether
there might be better or at least different

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:30.579
approaches to pair with that?

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:33.799
Speaker 4: Well, I agree with you that we've
got a problem.

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:39.350
And the reason that we are so divided as a
country is we're not using the same facts

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:44.470
to come to any conclusion, but at least we
have the same facts.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.570
We may differ about how we want to proceed
from there.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.659
But without that, it's bedlam.

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:57.609
And I'm inspired here by the work of Lee McIntyre,
a philosopher who's done a lot of work on

00:01:57.609 --> 00:01:58.609
this.

00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:01.390
And I'm with you and with Lee.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:08.630
You can't reason somebody out of a position
they didn't reason themselves into in the

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.220
first place.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:14.840
All you can do is listen, understand, and
make understood.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:18.140
Try to understand where they're coming from.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:23.420
Allow them not to feel judged by you.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.410
And then, as you say, ask questions about
how they came to believe, what they believed

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.720
and whether they think step by step.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.780
Is this a reliable source?

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.770
Is there another way to look at it?

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:41.980
And as as as MacIntyre says and as you said,
it's a very long and arduous process.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.120
But that's all we got for now.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.760
Speaker 1: There's no shortcuts.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.520
In other words, we've interviewed Lee, by
the way, on the program, and it was it was

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.520
fascinating.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:57.340
In your work overall over the years, you've
gone to the micro level of how graphs are

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.900
used to deceive and to lie.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:05.450
And you've also talked much more macro sort
of the way that that we just started today

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:13.040
for 2022, where it's rapid fire social media
sharing of fake articles and it's truncated

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.360
y axes.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.640
When you look at an unemployment rate or stock
market numbers, I mean, it's sort of like

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.600
an all of the above thing.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.130
Can we go a little narrower in specific about
what you see as some of the most commonly

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:25.960
used tools of deception?

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.560
Speaker 4: Yes, thank you.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.400
And we are currently doing some research on
this.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.980
And there may be some quicker ways to move
the needle a little bit that we can get to

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:37.980
later.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.300
Mm hmm.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.750
Distorting graphs is a big thing, as you mentioned,
a truncated y axis where, you know, the y

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.320
axis is the up and down one.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.470
And it looks like you've got a really big
difference, but it's zoomed in on a difference

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.700
that isn't really a difference to distort
it.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.500
People can say all kinds of things with graphs
that they didn't label the axes.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:06.800
If they're not labeled with numbers or what
the quantities are, you don't know anything.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.990
And my approach has been to educate.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:17.579
I'm an educator to try and educate people
about the different tricks and swindles.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:22.470
And really, I think the most effective thing
to do is to start with 12 year olds.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:27.680
I don't know that we're going to reach people
my age who have already made up their minds,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.680
maybe not even people your age, unless they're
especially open minded.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.620
But if we start with 12 year olds, in another
ten years, we'll have solved the problem.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.030
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.030
And you know what?

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:44.540
When one says that there is a particular group
that says you're talking about indoctrination,

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.650
when really you're talking about the opposite,
you're talking about a toolbox for people

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:53.620
to think for themselves rather than be indoctrinated
into any particular worldview.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:54.620
Is that correct?

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.170
Speaker 4: That's perfect.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.380
I've never sought to tell somebody what to
think.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:04.580
Just to teach them how to take a problem apart
and how to think about it and form their own

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:05.580
conclusions.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.960
And if they come up with a different conclusion
than I do, that's that's what democracy is

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.300
all about.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.300
I like you.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:18.360
I embrace that discussion as long as the process
was reasonable and somebody wasn't tricking

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:19.360
them.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.650
Speaker 1: When it comes a little more specifically
to news consumption, which, you know, we can

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.810
include radio, television and online kind
of all platforms.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:30.840
I mean, to use an analogy to what many of
us do when we look at an article or hear a

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.760
piece of news for the first time.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:38.210
One of the difficulties, it seems, in self-driving
vehicles is that there are some decisions

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:43.620
that we as humans make intuitively and quickly
that are really hard to have a computer mirror.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.920
A classic is, do you go through the yellow
light or do you stop?

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.170
And we're at the same time calculating how
fast are we going?

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.750
Someone behind us, the width of the intersection,
all of these different things.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:06:00.920
And we're making these decisions very, very
quickly in the same way many of us, myself

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:06.360
included, similarly make decisions when we
are first presented with a news article that

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.530
we see somewhere.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:13.210
That's fine, I think, to intuitively kind
of have that perception, but there really

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:20.190
is a probably better sequence or toolbox that
we can have to be able to assess for ourselves.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.370
Is a piece of news or article trustworthy?

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.280
Are the assertions accurate?

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:26.900
What would such a toolbox look like?

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.590
What would such a process be for the average
news consumer?

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:35.120
Speaker 4: Well, to begin with, I think we
can recognize can recognize, although we don't

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.810
all agree on this, that there are some people
who are experts in certain topics.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:44.360
So if you want to know about climate change,
talk to somebody who works in the field, somebody

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.000
who is a climate change scientist.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:51.930
Don't talk to a geneticist or a biologist.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:57.050
A lot of the climate change misinformation
has come from people who are not experts.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:02.410
And for that matter, if you want to know about
something going on in the brain, don't talk

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:03.610
to your foot doctor.

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:07.330
I think he or she might know, but it's less
likely.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:14.271
So I think the average person doesn't whoever
that average person is don't appreciate that.

00:07:14.271 --> 00:07:17.650
There really are experts who know things.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:24.450
Virologists is are an interesting case because
we've during the pandemic seen a lot of flip

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:26.060
flopping.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:29.910
And so people say, oh, well, they don't know
anything or they're there, they're just trying

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.390
to make money off of us.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:37.510
And the reality is, the second part of this
is to help people understand how science works,

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.570
because a lot of the information we consume
is scientific.

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:43.250
Science is a self-correcting process.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.180
They're flip flopping as they should.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.630
When new information comes in, they update
their views.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:55.770
The third thing we can do, I think, is understand
that there is a hierarchy of information sources.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:57.240
None is perfect.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:58.500
None is unbiased.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.940
All are biased and imperfect.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:07.380
But what you want in an information source
is one that will like science self-correct.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.380
Print a retraction.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:13.030
If it's wrong, check it sources before it
goes with the the article.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:18.690
So TMZ is a sort of favorite case of mine
because they did break the story of Michael

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:24.740
Jackson's death before anyone else, but they
got lucky for every story they broke.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:25.740
That was right.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.669
They broke 20 stories that were not right.

00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:31.470
They've said that Miley Cyrus was dead, all
kinds of stuff.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:38.039
And so I tend to trust sources that are a
little bit more deliberate and circumspect

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.449
and everybody has to draw the line somewhere
different.

00:08:40.449 --> 00:08:43.999
But I think we all agree there are sources
that.

00:08:43.999 --> 00:08:48.079
Ah, just kind of capriciously say what they
want.

00:08:48.079 --> 00:08:54.560
And included in those unfortunately are.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.970
I guess they're not really news shows.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:02.970
They're commentary shows, people commenting
on the news, which is not the news.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.029
Speaker 1: Right.

00:09:04.029 --> 00:09:06.930
And that goes to media literacy in a sense.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:11.010
I mean, it's being able to distinguish the
difference between news and opinion.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:15.230
Sometimes I'll get emails praising me for
being unbiased and I'll say, I love that you

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:21.779
love my show, but I am not a news show whose
bias we really should evaluate in those terms.

00:09:21.779 --> 00:09:24.579
I mean, this is a program about my opinion.

00:09:24.579 --> 00:09:29.620
The problem is that there are media actors
that are deliberately blurring that line.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:31.819
Speaker 4: Oh, for sure.

00:09:31.819 --> 00:09:35.279
It leads to better ratings if they say this
is what's really going on.

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:36.899
And let me tell you.

00:09:36.899 --> 00:09:38.720
They don't want you to know.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.339
And I have unique access to facts that you
will never encounter.

00:09:43.339 --> 00:09:45.830
That always makes me a little suspicious there.

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:49.959
Speaker 1: You mentioned climate change and
a couple of other things which I think lend

00:09:49.959 --> 00:09:52.749
themselves to to this next sort of idea.

00:09:52.749 --> 00:10:01.689
There's a contingent that has sort of placed
itself seemingly above the fray in terms of

00:10:01.689 --> 00:10:06.480
some of these partizan disagreements by taking
the position that they are.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.119
And this is my it's sort of a pejorative,
an enlightened centrist, in a sense.

00:10:10.119 --> 00:10:11.730
They've determined.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.709
Here's what one side thinks and here is what
the other side thinks.

00:10:14.709 --> 00:10:18.879
And I can rise above this and find the truth.

00:10:18.879 --> 00:10:21.170
And it happens to be sort of somewhere in
the middle.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:24.070
So if you watch Fox News, you watch MSNBC.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:25.920
And the truth is between those two.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.929
And of course, the problem with this is with
something like climate science, you know,

00:10:30.929 --> 00:10:35.069
if you say, well, let's bring in one person
who says it's real and one person who says

00:10:35.069 --> 00:10:36.069
it's not.

00:10:36.069 --> 00:10:38.360
And the truth is, it's sort of like in the
middle.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:42.629
This seems like like a problem, this idea
of the truth being in the middle, doesn't

00:10:42.629 --> 00:10:43.629
it?

00:10:43.629 --> 00:10:44.629
Speaker 4: I agree.

00:10:44.629 --> 00:10:49.629
There's not too look, there are two sides
to many stories, but there are not two sides

00:10:49.629 --> 00:10:50.629
to a story.

00:10:50.629 --> 00:10:57.879
If one side is a lie or a complete fabrication
or a distortion, that that's not a side amount

00:10:57.879 --> 00:11:02.569
that raises this thorny issue of who's going
to decide, right?

00:11:02.569 --> 00:11:04.069
What's a lie and what's not.

00:11:04.069 --> 00:11:05.769
And that gets back to where we started.

00:11:05.769 --> 00:11:10.580
I think each of us needs to be armed to make
that decision for ourselves.

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:11.580
We need a toolkit.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:16.330
And I you know, I've written a couple of books
that provide the toolkit, but lots of people

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.529
have I'm not here to hawk my books.

00:11:18.529 --> 00:11:23.679
There are a lot of books on critical thinking
and.

00:11:23.679 --> 00:11:25.670
It's a muscle that you have to exercise.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:32.220
You don't learn it once and then keep it because,
as you say, it goes against our evolutionary.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:36.250
Bias, which is to make snap decisions rather
than deliberate ones.

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:41.749
What my teacher Danny Kahneman, wrote about
in his book, Thinking Fast and Slow.

00:11:41.749 --> 00:11:48.689
And so this enlightened centrist, one of them
you may know of has called all sides dot com.

00:11:48.689 --> 00:11:49.689
Speaker 1: Hmm.

00:11:49.689 --> 00:11:50.689
Yeah.

00:11:50.689 --> 00:11:56.820
Speaker 4: Where he presents four different
views left to right, moderate left, moderate

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.749
right, an extreme left or an extreme right.

00:11:58.749 --> 00:12:02.699
And then you can decide the issue I have.

00:12:02.699 --> 00:12:07.970
And I told him, John Gable, is that some of
the stuff on both sides is really not fact

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:08.970
based.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.880
So it's not helpful.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.079
And they need to do a better job of sorting
that out.

00:12:14.079 --> 00:12:16.060
And again, I come back to.

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:17.079
Well.

00:12:17.079 --> 00:12:23.000
As a rule of thumb, I would ask some experts,
and if the experts disagree, that's fine.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:24.429
I would report that.

00:12:24.429 --> 00:12:29.269
But there are some things that experts really
don't disagree about.

00:12:29.269 --> 00:12:36.480
The the suitable experts will tell you that
the measles vaccine does not cause autism.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:37.480
Right.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.649
The information that was based on was retracted.

00:12:40.649 --> 00:12:43.600
The guy was found to have fabricated data.

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:46.649
Andrew Wakefield, he was his medical license
was taken away.

00:12:46.649 --> 00:12:50.060
I mean, that's about as strong a case as we
have.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:54.119
And yet people still believe it because there
are two sides, they think.

00:12:54.119 --> 00:12:58.600
Speaker 1: You mentioned Daniel Kahneman,
who wrote Thinking Fast and Slow.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:03.560
And much of much of the book is based on the
work he did with with Amos Swirsky.

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:07.850
One of the things that Daniel Kahneman writes
about is that so much of our everyday lives

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:13.899
is based on certain cognitive biases and heuristics
that we, many of us, unless you really take

00:13:13.899 --> 00:13:16.290
some time to learn about them, you don't understand
them.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:20.720
You know, availability, bias, you're very
much skewed to kind of what you see around

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:21.720
you.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:28.769
As one example, sometimes it's a bias to loss
aversion, even when a loss and an A gain can

00:13:28.769 --> 00:13:31.170
sort of be the same thing mathematically.

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:34.579
But depending on how it's presented, it feels
different.

00:13:34.579 --> 00:13:40.309
Which of those biases and heuristics do you
think are most relevant when it comes to news

00:13:40.309 --> 00:13:44.779
consumption and what we believe or disbelieve
or how we evaluate news?

00:13:44.779 --> 00:13:50.120
Speaker 4: You know, I worked in Condiments
Lab as a student and worked on some of the

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:51.730
experiments and.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:58.350
Kahneman I'm sorry, I work in first his lab
and Kahneman was around a lot, and Jaroussky

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:02.709
and Kahneman both said that.

00:14:02.709 --> 00:14:07.879
This stuff is so hard, you can teach it to
somebody and then they fall again a month

00:14:07.879 --> 00:14:08.879
later.

00:14:08.879 --> 00:14:11.040
And even PhDs and statistics were failing.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:13.629
Our little tests.

00:14:13.629 --> 00:14:16.769
Fiendishly difficult.

00:14:16.769 --> 00:14:22.740
I think we have to face the fact that we are
all biased, as you have admirably done.

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:28.180
We're biased towards, I mean, including trivial
non-news, where some people are biased towards

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:29.379
wanting to live in cities.

00:14:29.379 --> 00:14:31.970
Some people like to live in the country or
a suburb.

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:36.149
Some people want to take a vacation at the
beach, some in the mountains.

00:14:36.149 --> 00:14:38.379
Those are our biases or preferences.

00:14:38.379 --> 00:14:42.139
But hard to distinguish.

00:14:42.139 --> 00:14:45.850
When it comes to what we can arm ourselves
with.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:46.850
Yeah.

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:53.079
Versus an economy and have this inventory
of like 50 different things.

00:14:53.079 --> 00:15:01.040
Availability, heuristic, representativeness,
heuristic, the belief, perseverance, all these

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:02.800
things.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.079
I don't know that we need to learn those.

00:15:05.079 --> 00:15:06.629
That's a lot of work.

00:15:06.629 --> 00:15:07.629
Yeah.

00:15:07.629 --> 00:15:12.519
I mean, I find it entertaining, too, and I
would encourage anybody who wants to to do

00:15:12.519 --> 00:15:16.119
so because it sharpens your mind immeasurably.

00:15:16.119 --> 00:15:18.660
But from day to day.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:23.559
I don't I don't stop and think about that
stuff unless it pokes me in the face.

00:15:23.559 --> 00:15:29.019
It's such an obvious example of that bias,
either in reporting or in my own muddled thinking

00:15:29.019 --> 00:15:30.569
about something.

00:15:30.569 --> 00:15:36.440
What I really use is I look at the headline
and I ask myself if it's even plausible.

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:42.600
Does it does it just pass a simple plausibility
test?

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:47.059
And then I look at who's saying it and where
they got the information and that that search

00:15:47.059 --> 00:15:49.069
through about 80% of it for me.

00:15:49.069 --> 00:15:50.309
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:15:50.309 --> 00:15:57.189
And it's admittedly a very strange time because
I think five years ago a headline like the

00:15:57.189 --> 00:16:01.009
former president was flushing documents down
a toilet.

00:16:01.009 --> 00:16:04.689
We would all say it's not plausible and that
would be the end.

00:16:04.689 --> 00:16:06.220
But think things do change, right?

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:09.139
I mean, to some degree, we have to adjust
to the current circumstances.

00:16:09.139 --> 00:16:11.259
Speaker 4: Well, that's it.

00:16:11.259 --> 00:16:12.259
That's a wonderful point.

00:16:12.259 --> 00:16:15.620
There's this arcane thing we call Bayesian
inferencing.

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:16.620
Right.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:22.470
Which is just a fancy name that statisticians
and doctors and lawyers and judges use for.

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.999
If new information comes in, you put it on
the scale.

00:16:25.999 --> 00:16:34.560
So, you know, maybe the evidence for climate
change was that it was just a natural occurrence.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:38.559
Maybe if people thought that 40 years ago
and then information comes in and at some

00:16:38.559 --> 00:16:42.739
point the scales might be evenly balanced
or slightly tipped.

00:16:42.739 --> 00:16:46.310
And the idea is you have to stay open minded.

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:52.120
You have to be willing to learn something
new that may shake the very core of what you

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:53.120
believe.

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:59.359
Speaker 1: Is there a single question that
you could leave us with that is the most effective

00:16:59.359 --> 00:17:06.110
at starting conversations with people in order
to get them to have an open mind?

00:17:06.110 --> 00:17:15.230
Speaker 4: Some some version of Oh, I didn't
know that or I didn't realize that.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:17.720
Where did you learn that or how did you learn
that?

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:18.720
Right.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:22.679
And then just follow follow the question and
see where it goes.

00:17:22.679 --> 00:17:29.500
And again, don't try to shame somebody that
doesn't work.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:33.159
Don't try to say that their view is ridiculous.

00:17:33.159 --> 00:17:34.159
Right.

00:17:34.159 --> 00:17:37.100
Because they're going to shut down emotionally
and be defensive.

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:38.500
And we've seen the research.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:40.370
They'll double down.

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:46.409
But in our lab and in Gordon Penicuik lab
in Canada, a simple question of.

00:17:46.409 --> 00:17:50.330
Do you think that's reliable?

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:55.159
Or where did you learn that or, you know,
a lot of people say unreliable things.

00:17:55.159 --> 00:17:56.159
I don't know.

00:17:56.159 --> 00:17:57.190
Tell me, could this be one of them?

00:17:57.190 --> 00:17:58.190
Mm hmm.

00:17:58.190 --> 00:17:59.190
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:05.179
Another one that I think is interesting is
what would it mean for your worldview if this

00:18:05.179 --> 00:18:06.640
wasn't true?

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:11.640
And, you know, with the Trump was actually
the winner stuff?

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:17.710
There's a lot emotionally wrapped up in the
belief that Joe Biden didn't really win.

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:21.740
And not not everybody's willing to answer
the question, but for a lot of these folks,

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:28.530
it would be really impactful if their guy
just genuinely lost rather than there was

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:31.610
this major multi-state conspiracy to steal
it.

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:35.320
And but but not everybody's necessarily open
to taking that question seriously.

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:41.220
Speaker 4: Well, it's difficult, David, because
in one version of this story, what you have

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:46.570
to realize is, oh, a whole bunch of people
have been lying to me.

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:47.570
Right.

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:48.570
And I bought it.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:51.250
Am I that vulnerable and that gullible?

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:54.799
And am I that defenseless?

00:18:54.799 --> 00:19:00.260
What we see with older adults who get scammed
by con artists is they don't want to tell

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:02.120
anybody because they're embarrassed.

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:03.120
Right.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:06.940
And they kind of don't want to admit that
it was their fault for not asking the right

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:07.940
questions.

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:09.650
They just say, oh, well, this person lied
to me.

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:11.750
And it's true.

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:13.870
Some of those scammers are very, very good
liars.

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:18.159
But in government and media, we have some
very, very good liars, too.

00:19:18.159 --> 00:19:19.510
Speaker 1: That's true.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:20.510
That's true.

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:24.941
We've been speaking with Daniel Levitan, whose
books include A Field Guide to Lies, Critical

00:19:24.941 --> 00:19:29.010
Thinking with Statistics and the Scientific
Method and also Weaponised Lies How to think

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:31.380
critically in the post-truth era.

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:32.630
So appreciate your time today.

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:33.630
Thank you.

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.059
Speaker 4: Thank you, David.

00:19:35.059 --> 00:19:35.490
This is fun.

